ayers play optimally (Bazza maximizing the sum, Alice minimizing it), the state of the game follows a predictable trajectory.\n    *   **Lemma:** If neither player has won by turn $2m-2$ and both play optimally, Alice will choose $x_{2m-1} \\in \\{0, \\sqrt{2}\\}$ and Bazza will respond with $x_{2m}$ such that $x_{2m-1}^2+x_{2m}^2=2$. The sum increase over these two turns is $\\sqrt{2}$. This leads by induction to $Q_{2m}=2m$ and $S_{2m}=m\\sqrt{2}$.\n\n4.  **Determining the Winner:** We use the trajectory $S_{2m-2}=(m-1)\\sqrt{2}$ to determine when a player can break from the defensive equilibrium and force a win.\n    *   **Alice's Win ($\\lambda > \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$):** Alice can win on turn $2m-1$ if her budget allows her to play $x_{2m-1} > \\sqrt{2}$. This requires $\\lambda(2m-1) - S_{2m-2} > \\sqrt{2}$. Substituting $S_{2m-2}=(m-1)\\sqrt{2}$, the condition becomes $\\lambda > \\frac{m\\sqrt{2}}{2m-1}$. Since $\\lim_{m\\to\\infty} \\frac{m\\sqrt{2}}{2m-1} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, if $\\lambda > \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, Alice can always find a turn $2m-1$ to execute this winning move.\n    *   **Bazza's Win ($\\lambda < \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$):** Bazza wins if he can force $S_{2m-2} > \\lambda(2m-1)$ for some $m$. By always playing his sum-maximizing strategy, he ensures that the sum increase over any two turns is at least $\\sqrt{2}$, so $S_{2m-2} \\ge (m-1)\\sqrt{2}$. Bazza wins if $(m-1)\\sqrt{2} > \\lambda(2m-1)$, which is $\\lambda < \\frac{(m-1)\\sqrt{2}}{2m-1}$. Since $\\sup_{m\\ge 2} \\frac{(m-1)\\sqrt{2}}{2m-1} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, if $\\lambda < \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, Bazza can always force a win eventually.\n    *   **Draw ($\\lambda = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$):** At this threshold, neither of the winning inequalities can be satisfied. A specific sequence of moves is constructed where both players' constraints are always met, allowing the game to continue indefinitely.\n\n### 2. Detailed Solution\n\nLet $S_n = \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ and $Q_n = \\sum_{i