{2m-1} > \\lambda(2m-1)$. This is equivalent to $S_{2m-2} > \\lambda(2m-1)$. In this case, Bazza wins.\n*   Bazza loses on turn $2m$ if for all $x_{2m} \\ge 0$, $Q_{2m-1} + x_{2m}^2 > 2m$. This is equivalent to $Q_{2m-1} > 2m$. In this case, Alice wins.\n\n**Case 1: Alice has a winning strategy ($\\lambda > \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$)**\n\nThe function $f(m) = \\frac{m\\sqrt{2}}{2m-1}$ is decreasing for $m \\ge 1$ and $\\lim_{m\\to\\infty} f(m) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Since $\\lambda > \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, there exists a positive integer $m_0$ such that $\\lambda > f(m_0) = \\frac{m_0\\sqrt{2}}{2m_0-1}$.\n\nAlice's strategy is to play $x_{2k-1}=0$ for all $k < m_0$ and win on turn $2m_0-1$.\nOn turn $2m_0-1$, Alice can win if she can choose an $x_{2m_0-1} \\ge 0$ such that:\n1.  $S_{2m_0-2} + x_{2m_0-1} \\le \\lambda(2m_0-1)$\n2.  $Q_{2m_0-2} + x_{2m_0-1}^2 > 2m_0$\n\nThis is possible if and only if her maximum allowed move, $x_{max} = \\lambda(2m_0-1) - S_{2m_0-2}$, satisfies $Q_{2m_0-2} + x_{max}^2 > 2m_0$. This is equivalent to the condition:\n$$(\\lambda(2m_0-1) - S_{2m_0-2})^2 + Q_{2m_0-2} > 2m_0$$\nBazza's optimal defense is to choose his moves $x_2, x_4, \\dots, x_{2m_0-2}$ to minimize the expression $W = (\\lambda(2m_0-1) - S_{2m_0-2})^2 + Q_{2m_0-2}$, subject to his constraints $Q_{2k} \\le 2k$ for $k=1, \\dots, m_0-1$.\n\nLet $S = S_{2m_0-2} = \\sum_{k=1}^{m_0-1} x_{2k}$ and $Q = Q_{2m_0-2} = \\sum_{k=1}^{m_0-1} x_{2k}^2$. Bazza's final constraint is $Q \\le 2(m_0-1)$. By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, $S^2 \\le (m_0-1)Q$. To minimize $W$ for a fixed $Q$, Bazza should maximize $S$, so he sets $S = \\sqrt{(m_0-1)Q}$. This is achieved by setting $x_2=x_4=\\dots=x_{2m_0-2}$.\nBazza's problem reduces to choosing $Q \\in [0, 2(m_0-1)]$ to minimize $h(Q) = (\\lambda(2m_0-1) - \\sqrt{(m_0-1)Q})^2 + Q$.\nExpanding this, $h(Q) = (\\lambda(2m_0-1))^2 - 2\\lambda(2m_0-1)\\sqrt{m_0-1}Q^{1/2} + m_0Q$.\nThe derivative is $h'(Q) = m_0 - \\frac{\\lambda(2m_0-1)\\sqrt{m_0-1}