r loses if they cannot make a valid move.\n*   Alice loses on turn $2m-1$ if for all $x_{2m-1} \\ge 0$, $S_{2m-2} + x_{2m-1} > \\lambda(2m-1)$. This is equivalent to $S_{2m-2} > \\lambda(2m-1)$. In this case, Bazza wins.\n*   Bazza loses on turn $2m$ if for all $x_{2m} \\ge 0$, $Q_{2m-1} + x_{2m}^2 > 2m$. This is equivalent to $Q_{2m-1} > 2m$. In this case, Alice wins.\n\n**Case 1: Alice has a winning strategy ($\\lambda > \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$)**\n\nThe function $f(m) = \\frac{m\\sqrt{2}}{2m-1}$ is decreasing for $m \\ge 1$ since $f'(m) = \\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{(2m-1)^2} < 0$. Also, $\\lim_{m\\to\\infty} f(m) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Since $\\lambda > \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, there exists a positive integer $m_0$ such that $\\lambda > f(m_0) = \\frac{m_0\\sqrt{2}}{2m_0-1}$.\n\nAlice's strategy is to play $x_{2k-1}=0$ for all $k < m_0$ and win on turn $2m_0-1$.\n\n**Validity of Alice's setup moves:**\nFor any $k \\in \\{1, \\dots, m_0-1\\}$, Alice's move $x_{2k-1}=0$ is valid if $S_{2k-2} \\le \\lambda(2k-1)$. With Alice's strategy, $S_{2k-2} = \\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} x_{2j}$. Bazza's moves are constrained by $Q_{2k-2} = \\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} x_{2j}^2 \\le 2(k-1)$. By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, $S_{2k-2}^2 \\le (k-1)Q_{2k-2} \\le 2(k-1)^2$, so $S_{2k-2} \\le (k-1)\\sqrt{2}$.\nAlice's move is valid if $(k-1)\\sqrt{2} \\le \\lambda(2k-1)$. This is equivalent to $\\lambda \\ge \\frac{(k-1)\\sqrt{2}}{2k-1}$. The function $h(k) = \\frac{(k-1)\\sqrt{2}}{2k-1}$ is increasing for $k \\ge 1$ and its limit is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Thus, $h(k) \\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ for all $k \\ge 1$. Since $\\lambda > \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, the condition $\\lambda > h(k)$ is always satisfied. So Alice's setup moves are always valid.\n\n**Alice's winning move on turn $2m_0-1$:**\nAlice can win if she can choose an $x_{2m_0-1} \\ge 0$ such that $S_{2m_0-2} + x_{2m_0-1} \\le \\lambda(2m_0-1)$ and $Q_{2m_0-2} + x_{2m_0-1}^2 > 2m_0$. This is possible if her maximum allowed move, $x_{max} = \\lambda(2m_